**/obj/
*.user
*.suo
# Rust
target/
# Node / Vite
node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/.vite/
# Node / Nuxt
# The public Nuxt site is developed as its own repository and is INTENDED to be a submodule here.
# It is not one yet — as of 2026-09-22 there is no `website/.git`, no `.gitmodules`, and git holds
# no gitlink for it; it is a plain directory of files. Ignoring it is therefore the interim, and
# the reason is not tidiness. The whole directory is ignored, not just its build output: until
# 2026-09-22 only the three build directories below were listed, so an ordinary `git add .` here
# would have swallowed ~114 of the site's source files into the panel's repository. CLAUDE.md and
# `maran website` already referenced the site while git held nothing of it, which is the shape of
# the mistake this line prevents.
#
# IT IS A PRIVATE REPOSITORY (the source of maran.innovayse.com), and that decides more than where
# it lives. Maran itself is source-available: anyone may clone it. A private submodule inside a
# public repository fails for every one of them — `git clone --recursive` stops on a permission
# error, and `git submodule update` does too, with a message about authentication that reads like a
# broken repository rather than a deliberate boundary. So registering it as a submodule here would
# trade a clean clone for everyone against a convenience for the few who can fetch it. Ignoring it,
# as now, keeps both repositories clonable by the people entitled to each.
#
# IF IT IS REGISTERED ANYWAY: `git submodule add` refuses a path that is ignored — remove this entry
# first or pass `-f`, rather than concluding the tooling is broken — and expect the public clone
# question above to need an answer, such as marking the submodule optional.
website/
# Playwright
frontend/test-results/
frontend/playwright-report/
frontend/blob-report/
frontend/.playwright/
# Scratch output from a browser-driven session (screenshots, snapshots, console logs).
.playwright-mcp/

# The same scratch, when the browser tool writes it beside the working directory instead of
# into its own folder. One such screenshot reached `dev` in #18: it was swept up by a `git add
# -A` because nothing here matched it, and 95 KB of a login screen is now in the history for
# good. An image belongs in this repository only when something renders it, and nothing does.
/*.png
/*.jpeg
/*.webp

# The same shape again, for probes rather than screenshots. A reviewer checking what
# `"nan".parse::<f64>()` really does wrote `nan.rs` at the repository root, compiled it, and left
# both behind. No Rust source and no built binary belongs here — every crate lives under `agent/`,
# and a loose one at the root is scratch that a `git add -A` would sweep into the history exactly
# as #18's screenshot was. Probes belong in the session scratchpad.
/*.rs

# Generated code
# --------------
# Protobuf and gRPC types are generated at build time on both sides and never committed
# (rules/proto.md).
**/*.g.cs
**/generated/
# Wolverine writes a handler type per message under Internal/Generated at runtime.
**/Internal/Generated/

# Secrets and machine-local configuration
# ---------------------------------------
# Real values never enter the repository; the *.example files document every variable
# (rules/security.md).
.env
.env.*
docker/.env
*.local.json
appsettings.*.local.json
!.env.example
!docker/.env.example
!installer/panel.env.example
# Keys and certificates, wherever they land
*.pem
# …except pebble's own test CA root, which is PUBLIC by construction: it signs only the
# throwaway directory of a local test ACME server, ships inside pebble's public image, and
# is pinned by an integration test that cannot work without it. A clone missing this file
# fails that test for a reason no error message would explain.
!docker/pebble/pebble-minica.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
!**/*.pem.example

# One exception, and it is narrow on purpose: the agent's SSL tests read fixture certificates at
# COMPILE time via include_str!, so without them in the repository `cargo test` does not fail — it
# does not build at all, and only on a clean checkout. That is what happened: the suite was green on
# every developer machine and CI could not compile it.
#
# These are self-signed certificates for example.com, the domain RFC 2606 reserves for exactly this,
# minted for the suite and belonging to no host and no authority. The rule above still catches every
# real key, including anything an operator or a test run drops elsewhere in the tree.
!agent/crates/ops/tests/fixtures/*.pem

# installer/keys/: installer/lib/50-artifacts.sh:28 loads release-signing.pub from here and it
# MUST ship with the installer package, so it is not covered by the blanket *.pem/*.key rule above.
# Only private signing material is excluded — scripts/lib/release-bundle.sh never writes a private
# key into this directory (it takes --key/$MARAN_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY from OUTSIDE the repository),
# so these patterns exist to catch a human mistake, not tooling output.
installer/keys/*private*
installer/keys/*.key
!installer/keys/release-signing.pub

